From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dhaval Gujar <dhaval.gujar@espressif.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 04:29:41 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] Add ggl-svc, a service manager for builds without systemd

ggl-svcd runs the core daemons and the units ggdeploymentd generates for
components. It implements the subset of systemd unit semantics they use:
Type, NotifyAccess, ExecStart, User, Group, WorkingDirectory, Environment,
Restart, RestartSec, RemainAfterExit, SuccessExitStatus, start limits
(StartLimitIntervalSec/StartLimitBurst), start and stop timeouts,
After/Wants ordering and BindsTo (a unit stops, or restarts, with the unit
it is bound to), with systemd's defaults. As under systemd, each unit runs
in its own cgroup (process grouping only, which needs CONFIG_CGROUPS and no
controller): stopping a unit sends SIGTERM to every process in it and
SIGKILL after the stop timeout, and processes are mapped to their unit by
their cgroup. Unit output goes to syslog. Readiness of the core daemons is
the appearance of their core-bus socket (X-GGL-ReadySocket), in place of
socket activation.

State persists in a root-only directory, including a copy of each linked
unit file taken when it is linked, which is what runs: a unit file left in
a directory the core daemons' user can write cannot later be replaced by
one that runs as root. The control socket accepts state-changing requests
from root only, reads requests without blocking and limits how many
connections each non-root user can hold open; clients give up on a call
that is not answered within 10 seconds.

A systemctl front end forwards the commands ggdeploymentd issues (link,
enable, disable, start, stop, restart, daemon-reload, reset-failed,
reboot); start, restart and stop wait for the job unless --no-block is
given. A systemd-notify front end notifies the caller's own unit, which
the startup phase scripts recipe-runner generates rely on; as under
systemd, only Type=notify units accept notifications.

gghealthd gets a matching backend: lifecycle states, restarts,
systemd-notify style state updates and lifecycle subscriptions come from
ggl-svcd instead of systemd's D-Bus API, with the same state mapping.

ggipc-auth asks ggl-svcd which unit an IPC peer belongs to, in place of
systemd's cgroup-based sd_pid_get_unit(), and derives the component name
from it exactly as before.

Only built with GGL_PORTABLE_CORE=ON.

Signed-off-by: Dhaval Gujar <dhaval.gujar@espressif.com>
Upstream: not yet submitted (developed on the esp32s31/v2.7.0 branch of a fork)
